Optical Character Recognition (OCR) technology has fundamentally transformed how we interact with textual information in the digital age. From its humble beginnings in the early 20th century, when primitive machines could barely recognize a few standardized characters, OCR has evolved into a sophisticated field combining computer vision, machine learning, natural language processing, and computational linguistics. Today's OCR systems can handle an astounding variety of text types, from pristine printed documents to degraded historical manuscripts, from perfectly aligned text to skewed images captured on mobile phones.

The journey of OCR development mirrors the broader evolution of artificial intelligence. Early systems relied on template matching, where each character was compared against a database of known character templates. This approach worked adequately for documents using standard fonts at consistent sizes, but failed catastrophically when confronted with font variations, size changes, or any form of distortion. The next generation introduced feature-based methods, which extracted geometric and topological features from character shapes and used these for classification. While more robust than pure template matching, these methods still required extensive manual feature engineering and struggled with the enormous